-### DIB-R
-A differentiable rasterization method that uses a soft rasterization approach with a stochastic sampling mechanism, allowing gradients to flow to occluded triangles, unlike NMR's z-culling. [DIB-R](6798-dib-r.md)
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Z-culling:** Gradients do not flow to occluded triangles. This saves computation but may hinder geometry optimization if occluded geometry is relevant [S1][S3].
+*   **Discrete Boundaries:** The gradient is zero if the loss gradient and color change direction are aligned (i.e., movement does not help minimize loss) [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** Linear interpolation approximation may introduce artifacts near boundaries if not carefully implemented [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not explicitly given, but Jrender's optimized implementation runs about ten times faster than the official implementation [S1, S3].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Standard rasterization buffers plus overhead for gradient computation [G].
-*   **Performance:** On a 280-triangle bowl at 256x256 resolution, Jrender's NMR takes 7.5ms for forward rendering plus gradient, compared to 8.1ms for the official NMR [S1, S3].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles the localness issue of OpenDR; supports fragment-level shading; objective-aware gradients; efficient with z-culling [S1][S2, unofficial][S3].
-*   **Limitations:** Gradients do not flow to occluded triangles due to z-culling; may struggle with complex mutual occlusions compared to probabilistic methods like SoftRas [S1, S3].
-*   **When to use:** General purpose differentiable mesh rendering where efficiency and boundary gradient flow are important [S1, S3].
-*   **When not to use:** When gradients to occluded geometry are critical for optimization, consider SoftRas instead [S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of pixels and faces. Jrender's optimized implementation is ~10x faster than the official implementation [S1][S3].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to mesh size and image resolution.
+*   **Performance:** On a 280-triangle bowl at 256x256 resolution, Jrender's NMR takes 7.5ms (forward + gradient) vs 8.1ms for official NMR [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Supports fragment shading (textures, complex lighting); non-local gradients avoid local minima; efficient z-culling [S1][S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Cannot optimize occluded geometry; approximation may be inaccurate for complex mutual occlusions compared to SoftRas [S1][S3].
+*   **When to use:** General-purpose differentiable mesh rendering, single-image 3D reconstruction, style transfer [S1][S4, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When gradients to occluded geometry are critical (use SoftRas) [S3].
 
 ## Background
-NMR was proposed by Kato et al. [9] to overcome the limitations of OpenDR, specifically the localness of gradient computation and the lack of objective awareness [S2, unofficial]. It preserves traditional rasterization for the forward pass while approximating gradients for the backward pass [S1, S3]. The method has been integrated into applications such as single image 3D reconstruction and 3D mesh style editing [S1, S3]. An optimized implementation in Jrender achieves approximately ten times the speed of the original [S1, S3].
+*   **Origin:** Introduced by Kato et al. in 2017 (arXiv:1711.07566) and published at CVPR 2018 [S4, unofficial].
+*   **History:** Developed to overcome limitations of OpenDR, specifically localness and lack of objective awareness [S2, unofficial]. It integrates 3D mesh rendering into neural networks for tasks like 3D reconstruction and editing [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Usage:** Single-image 3D reconstruction, 3D mesh style editing, 3D Deep Dream [S1][S4, unofficial].
 
